1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:09,700
Published policy at national archives and libraries points to one pattern: microfilm stays the preservation master, and digital copies serve access.

2
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:18,360
Silver-halide film on a polyester base, processed and stored to standard, is rated LE-500 to ISO 18901.

3
00:00:18,710 --> 00:00:24,330
It needs only light and magnification to read, and no format migration.

4
00:00:24,830 --> 00:00:30,630
Film in a vault is not on a network, so ransomware and remote tampering cannot reach it.

5
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:37,100
Keep master and duplicate negatives in different buildings, so no single disaster takes both.

6
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:39,910
Best practice is three generations.

7
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:42,900
The camera negative is never circulated.

8
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:46,080
A print master is used only to make copies.

9
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:48,610
Service copies go to researchers.

10
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:55,940
NARA, the Library of Congress and the British Library all separate preservation masters from service copies.

11
00:00:56,290 --> 00:01:02,290
The hybrid approach scans a service negative for digital access, leaving the masters untouched.

12
